    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm studying for sec+ and i'm kinda stuck on one thing. if the password is already being hashed, why do we even need to salt it? wouldnt the hash already make it secure anyway?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Hashing makes data irreversible, but it doesn't prevent attacks like rainbow tables. Salting adds a unique, random string to each password *before* hashing, ensuring the same password results in a different hash every time. This effectively defeats pre-computed attacks because each unique salt requires a completely new hash calculation. Therefore, salting significantly increases the effort required for an attacker to crack passwords.",
      "source": "target",
