    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok i get that now. so like, if an attacker gets the database, they see the salt and the hash. if they know the salt, can't they just add it to their rainbow table and crack it anyway? how does that actually stop them?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The key is that the salt is unique to *each individual user*, not shared across all passwords. An attacker cannot pre-calculate a single rainbow table that works against every salted hash. Since each password uses a different salt, they would have to generate a unique, complex attack vector for every single user record. This forces the attacker to perform slow, individual brute-force cracking for every password, making the attack computationally infeasible.",
      "source": "target",
