    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so it's basically like making the work too much for them. what if they use massive computing power though? like, can't they just automate the salted brute force to keep up with all the different users?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While massive computing power helps, salting combined with strong hashing algorithms like bcrypt or Argon2 dramatically slows down the rate at which passwords can be checked. These modern hashing functions are deliberately designed to be computationally *expensive* (slow by design), meaning processing one hash takes significant time. This inherent delay, even when automated across many CPUs, makes cracking millions of passwords prohibitively slow and expensive for an attacker.",
